**Project summary:** The DriverPass project centered on a fictional driver-training company that wanted to move away from paper-based scheduling and manual recordkeeping toward a fully online system. DriverPass, the client, needed a platform that would let customers register, schedule and pay for lessons, and track their own progress, while giving staff a way to manage instructor schedules, student records, and communication with the DMV. Throughout the course, this case study served as the foundation for a System Design Document and Client Presentation, supported by UML use case, activity, sequence, and class diagrams that modeled how each type of user would interact with the new system.

**What I did well:** I think my strongest work was in translating the client's fairly loose, narrative description of their needs into structured, unambiguous UML diagrams. The use case diagram in particular held up well throughout the project because I took the time early on to clearly separate the actors — Customer, Secretary, IT Officer, Owner, and the DMV System — and define exactly how each one interacted with the system boundary. That early clarity made the later activity and sequence diagrams much easier to build consistently, since I wasn't rediscovering the same logic each time. I also felt good about how the Gantt chart and Business Requirements Document worked together to keep the technical design grounded in a realistic project timeline, which is something that's easy to lose sight of when you're deep in diagramming.

**What I'd revise:** If I could revisit one piece, I'd go back to the activity diagram, specifically the DMV verification and payment-processing steps. Looking back, I modeled the "happy path" more thoroughly than I modeled exceptions and failure states — what happens if a payment is declined, or if the DMV system times out or returns invalid data. A stronger version of that diagram would include decision points and alternate flows for those failure cases, since real systems spend a lot of their design effort handling exactly those situations. I'd also tighten up the labeling on some of the swimlanes so it's immediately clear which actor is responsible for each step without needing to cross-reference the narrative description.

**Interpreting user needs:** I approached this by working backward from each actor's goals rather than starting with the system's technical structure. For the Customer, that meant asking what they actually need to accomplish — scheduling a lesson, checking progress, making a payment — and making sure the use case and sequence diagrams reflected that experience step by step rather than just the backend logic. For staff like the Secretary or Owner, I focused on what information they'd need visibility into and how much manual work the system should be automating away. This matters because a system that's technically sound but doesn't match how real users actually think and work will get rejected or misused no matter how well it's engineered. Good requirements gathering and user-centered modeling are really what prevent expensive redesigns later — it's much cheaper to catch a mismatch in a UML diagram than after the system is built.

**My approach to software design:** My general strategy is to move from broad to specific: start with use cases to understand who's involved and what they need, then layer in activity diagrams to map out process flow, sequence diagrams to nail down the timing and communication between components, and class diagrams last, once the behavior is well understood, so the data structures actually support the behavior rather than the other way around. Going forward, I'd like to be more disciplined about building out alternate and exception flows from the start rather than treating them as an afterthought, and I'd also want to get more comfortable validating diagrams against stakeholder feedback early, since a lot of design flaws are easier to catch in conversation than in a finished diagram.

